const e={note:"The Theatrum Chemicum is the great Strasbourg anthology of Latin alchemy, assembled by the bookseller Lazarus Zetzner: three volumes appeared in 1602, printed at Oberursel by Cornelius Sutorius at Zetzner's charge, a fourth followed in 1613 and a fifth in 1622; the whole was reissued at Strasbourg by the heirs of Eberhard Zetzner from 1659, in six volumes, the sixth appearing in 1661. By then the collection had become the largest body of Latin alchemical writing ever put into print. Its importance is not only its bulk but its rescue work: for a good many of the pieces it gathers, Zetzner's sheets remain the most accessible — and in some cases the only — text a reader can consult. Jung worked from it constantly, citing it by volume and page throughout Psychologie und Alchemie, and his footnotes are the route by which many of these titles reached readers of English at all. It should be said plainly that most of the treatises listed here have never been translated into English, and several have attracted very little scholarship. What the Scriptorium offers is a way in — a true title, an author where there is one, the genuine opening words, and a verified scan of the printed page — and not a substitute for the Latin.",works:[{slug:"de-alchemiae-difficultatibus",native:"De alchemiae difficultatibus liber",translit:null,english:"A Book on the Difficulties of Alchemy",author:"Theobald van Hoghelande (Theobaldus de Hoghelande), of Middelburg in Zeeland, c. 1560 – c. 1608. The Theatrum's head-title styles him 'Mittelburgensis'.",date:"First printed at Cologne, 1594; gathered into the Theatrum Chemicum, vol. I",language:"Latin",volume:"Theatrum Chemicum, vol. I",description:"Hoghelande writes for the practitioner who has already failed. His subject is not the theory of the stone but the specific ways in which the work goes wrong — mistaken matter, mistaken vessel, mistaken fire, and above all the deliberate obscurity of the authorities, many of whom he quotes and collates against one another. The full title promises to teach 'what the student of the true chemistry, aspiring to perfection, ought to know and what he ought to avoid', and the book keeps that promise with unusual literalness: it is a catalogue of difficulties, drawn from a reading of the whole Latin corpus. A second Hoghelande, Ewald, wrote on the same subjects in the same years and is easily confused with him.",incipit:{native:"Naturalis philosophiae partem nobilissimam esse, qua metallorum tractantur principia, causae, proprietates, passiones, & transmutationes, nemo nisi injuria, in dubium revocabit.",english:"That the noblest part of natural philosophy is that in which the principles, causes, properties, affections and transmutations of metals are treated, no one will call into doubt except unjustly.",note:"The opening sentence of the Prooemium claims the highest rank for the subject, before the book turns for the whole of its remaining length to everything that can go wrong within it."},contents:null,contentsNote:"The book has no chapter divisions of any kind — no capita, sections or numbered parts occur anywhere in it. After a short Prooemium it runs as one continuous discourse, its argument marked only by the dense printed side-notes in the margin, which carry the running citations to the authorities and serve the reader in place of a table of contents.",editions:[{label:"Theatrum Chemicum, vol. I (Strasbourg: heirs of Eberhard Zetzner, 1659)",url:"https://archive.org/details/BIUSante_pharma_res011287x01/page/n113/mode/1up",note:"The Paris medical faculty's copy, digitised by the Bibliothèque interuniversitaire de santé. The link opens at the treatise's head-title and the first page of the Prooemium; the whole volume may be read online or downloaded."},{label:"Theatrum Chemicum, vol. I (1659) — a second copy",url:"https://archive.org/details/theatrumchemicu00habrgoog/page/n119/mode/1up",note:"A different scan of the same first volume, from the Ghent University copy, useful where the Paris copy is faint. It opens at the same head-title."},{label:"De alchemiae difficultatibus (Cologne: Heinrich Falckenburg, 1594)",url:"https://archive.org/details/hin-wel-all-00001220-001",note:"The separate first edition, from the Wellcome Library copy: fourteen preliminary leaves and 165 pages, octavo, with printed marginalia and a printer's device on the title-page."}]},{slug:"dialogus-inter-naturam-et-filium-philosophiae",native:"Dialogus inter naturam et filium philosophiae",translit:null,english:"A Dialogue Between Nature and the Son of Philosophy",author:"Aegidius de Vadis (fl. 1521). Nothing is known of him beyond this dialogue, which he addresses to an unnamed friend, 'N.'",date:"Composed 1521 — the dedicatory letter is dated from the royal library at Richmond — and printed in the Theatrum Chemicum, 1602",language:"Latin",volume:"Theatrum Chemicum, vol. II",description:"Nature appears in person to a seeker who confesses himself unlettered and a stammerer, tells him not to be afraid — 'Noli timere fili, ego sum Natura' — and consents to answer his questions one at a time; what follows is a catechism of the whole art in twenty-two chapters, from the matter of the stone and the hermaphrodite nature of minerals to the squaring of the circle and the vegetative spirit that moves things into being. Because the dialogue form obliges Nature to answer plainly, it is among the most lucid of the teaching texts in the collection — the tenth chapter even concedes that the science 'is a part of the Kabbalah, and can be taught in the ear'. In the Theatrum it is preceded by a preface to the reader from Bernard Gilles Penot, who defends the publishing of such mysteries while noting that he is not their author.",incipit:{native:"Filius. Iam ergo Domina, postquam Deo meo pro omnibus beneficiis suis gratias egi, interrogabo te, si vero libuerit respondebis. — Natura. Propone fili, & cum Dei adiutorio tuis dubiis satisfaciam.",english:"The Son: Now then, my Lady, having given thanks to my God for all his benefits, I shall put my questions to you — and you will answer, if it please you. Nature: Set them out, my son, and with God's help I shall satisfy your doubts.",note:"The first exchange of the dialogue proper, in Caput I; the dedicatory letter, the author's Praefatio and a prayer to God stand before it."},contents:[{heading:"Caput I. In quo Natura docet filium artis, quaenam sit materia lapidis: dicens, quod ex fine intentionis resultat principium operis.",english:"Chapter I. In which Nature teaches the son of the art what the matter of the stone is, saying that the beginning of the work follows from the end one intends."},{heading:"Caput II. In hoc secundo capite natura dicit mineralia esse hermaphrodita natura, eo quod utrunque sexum habent, scilicet sulphur & argentum vivum. Et quod incrementum alicuius rei pendet à suo principio.",english:"Chapter II. In this second chapter Nature says that minerals are hermaphrodite by nature, since they have both sexes — that is, sulphur and quicksilver; and that the growth of anything depends upon its beginning."},{heading:"Caput III. Natura docet, quaenam sit illa materia operi physico necessaria, & exemplo probat ut videre licet legendo hoc caput tertium.",english:"Chapter III. Nature teaches what that matter is which the physical work requires, and proves it by an example, as may be seen on reading this third chapter."},{heading:"Caput IV. Quid sit chaos, & est materia illa arti necessaria. Materia etiam lapidis est propensa ad omnes formas suscipiendas: & palam venditur.",english:"Chapter IV. What chaos is, and that it is the matter the art requires. The matter of the stone is also disposed to receive every form; and it is sold openly."},{heading:"Caput V. In isto opere physico non capitur id ex quo metalla sunt, adducens exemplum de creatione hominis: item quomodo res in visceribus terrae circulando generantur.",english:"Chapter V. That in this physical work one does not take that out of which the metals are made — with the example of the creation of man; and how things are generated by circulation in the bowels of the earth."},{heading:"Caput VI. Natura docet artis filium se non posse facere materiam, ex qua Mercurius Philosophorum fit, & quare vocatur Mercurius Philosophorum.",english:"Chapter VI. Nature teaches the son of the art that he cannot himself make the matter out of which the Mercury of the Philosophers is made, and why it is called the Mercury of the Philosophers."},{heading:"Caput VII. Ibi Natura docet rem omnem habere suum contrarium. Ultra ubi est attractio, ibi erit & retentio, & quod attractio ferri à magnete artem manifestat.",english:"Chapter VII. Here Nature teaches that every thing has its contrary. Further, that where there is attraction there will also be retention; and that the drawing of iron by the lodestone makes the art plain."},{heading:"Caput VIII. Quid sit depuratio, & sine illa vix quicquam boni operabitur in hac arte, quia totum magisterium in materiae praeparatione consistit, & quod Aurum vulgi non sit lapidis materia.",english:"Chapter VIII. What purification is, and that without it scarcely anything good will be done in this art, since the whole mastery consists in the preparation of the matter; and that common gold is not the matter of the stone."},{heading:"Caput IX. Necessario in hac arte duo necessaria esse, nempe agens & patiens: Quia unum & simplex non agit in seipsum.",english:"Chapter IX. That two things are of necessity required in this art, namely an agent and a patient; because what is one and simple does not act upon itself."},{heading:"Caput X. Haec scientia est pars cabalae, & potest doceri in aure, modo quis sciat portionem ponderum.",english:"Chapter X. That this science is a part of the Kabbalah, and can be taught in the ear, provided one knows the proportion of the weights."},{heading:"Caput XI. Quomodo Natura regit omnia, tam inferiora quam superiora, certis mensuris & rotationibus elementorum.",english:"Chapter XI. How Nature governs all things, lower as well as higher, by fixed measures and revolutions of the elements."},{heading:"Caput XII. In hac arte radices metallorum sunt cognoscendae. Cognita hac scientia caetera scientia facile cognoscentur. Idcirco Deus omnium scientiarum donator orandus est.",english:"Chapter XII. That in this art the roots of the metals must be known. Once this science is known, the other sciences will be known easily. Therefore God, the giver of all sciences, must be prayed to."},{heading:"Caput XIIII. In numeris omnia sita sunt. Imo elementa certis numeris ligantur.",english:"Chapter XIIII. That all things are set in numbers. Indeed the elements themselves are bound by fixed numbers."},{heading:"Caput XV. Haec scientia est investigatrix rerum naturalium, qua pervenitur ad cognitionem abditarum rerum.",english:"Chapter XV. That this science is the searcher-out of natural things, by which one comes to the knowledge of hidden things."},{heading:"Caput XVI. Quatuor sunt gradus in rerum natura & non plures, & quod aurum est ultimus appetitus materiae & formae.",english:"Chapter XVI. That there are four degrees in the nature of things and no more; and that gold is the last desire of matter and of form."},{heading:"Caput XVII. Adhuc scientiam graduum prosequitur, & quod gradus unus alium superat.",english:"Chapter XVII. He pursues the science of the degrees further, and shows that one degree surpasses another."},{heading:"Caput XVIII. Hic docet quid sit quadratura circuli, quam multi periti frustra quaerunt, ut Aristotelem non intelligunt, varia tractantur in hoc capite.",english:"Chapter XVIII. Here he teaches what the squaring of the circle is, which many skilled men seek in vain, inasmuch as they do not understand Aristotle; various matters are treated in this chapter."},{heading:"Caput XIX. Haec scientia non acquiritur receptis aut vanis studiis, sed laborando, meditando, & re ipsa praestando.",english:"Chapter XIX. That this science is not got by recipes or by idle study, but by labouring, meditating, and performing the thing itself."},{heading:"Caput XX. Docet quid sit primum movens in rebus naturalibus. Quomodo ex uno grano seminato natura multiplicat in infinitum. Sic & Ars facit imitatione naturae; Et absoluto lapide poteris ad talem formam ducere qualem volueris.",english:"Chapter XX. He teaches what the first mover in natural things is; and how, from a single sown grain, nature multiplies without end. Art does the same in imitation of nature; and when the stone is finished you may bring it to whatever form you wish."},{heading:"Caput XXI. Medicina pharmacopaeorum sunt corruptibiles, Medicina physicorum incorruptibilis; Lapis multiplicatur multitudine spirituum.",english:"Chapter XXI. The medicines of the apothecaries are corruptible, the medicine of the natural philosophers incorruptible; the stone is multiplied by a multitude of spirits."},{heading:"Caput XXII. Digestio fit beneficio caloris humidi, & non sufficit in opere calorem externum habere, sed illum qui in materia delitescit.",english:"Chapter XXII. That digestion is brought about by a moist heat; and that in the work it is not enough to have an outward heat, but that heat which lies hidden within the matter."},{heading:"Caput XXIII. Natura docet rem omnem esse cum anima, id est, spiritu vegetabili, qui quidem Spiritus movet res ad esse. Item hanc artem, arbitrii nostri non esse sed Dei omnipotentis: Ideo invocandus & orandus est.",english:"Chapter XXIII. Nature teaches that every thing has a soul — that is, a vegetative spirit — and that this Spirit moves things into being. Likewise that this art lies not at our own disposal but at almighty God's, and that he is therefore to be invoked and prayed to."}],contentsNote:"The dialogue is divided into chapters, each headed by a summary rubric which the compositor set in italic beneath the chapter number. The 1602 printing numbers them from I to XXIII but has no chapter XIII, passing straight from the twelfth to the fourteenth, so that there are twenty-two in all; the headings above are given as printed, and the numbering is left as the printer left it.",editions:[{label:"Theatrum Chemicum, vol. II (Oberursel: Cornelius Sutorius for Lazarus Zetzner, 1602)",url:"https://archive.org/details/BIUSante_pharma_res011287x02/page/n103/mode/1up",note:"The link opens at the dialogue's title, with the close of Penot's preface standing above it on the same page. The dedicatory letter from Richmond follows overleaf, and the first chapter begins at scan image 106."}]},{slug:"liber-de-magni-lapidis-compositione-et-operatione",native:"Liber de magni lapidis compositione et operatione",translit:null,english:"A Book on the Composition and Operation of the Great Stone",author:"Anonymous. The 1602 head-title reads 'auctore adhuc incerto, sed tamen doctissimo' — the author as yet unknown, but most learned all the same; the running head throughout is simply 'Incerti auctoris'.",date:"Medieval; printed in the Theatrum Chemicum, 1602",language:"Latin",volume:"Theatrum Chemicum, vol. III",description:"The treatise that opens the third volume, and one of the fullest practical expositions in the whole collection. It begins with the qualifications required of the artificer — a most subtle wit, sufficient means, sound hands and eyes, patience, and constancy of purpose — insists that books alone will never teach the art, since the philosophers wrote 'obscurely, and in various riddles, metaphorically and figuratively', and then proceeds through more than fifty short chapters of water, earth, elixir and stone. Its authorities are the old Latin-Arabic ones — Geber, Morienus, Alphidius, Aros, Hermes, Hortulanus — which places it in the medieval tradition rather than the Paracelsian, and it states outright that the science was granted by God for the poor alone, adding that if kings and princes knew it no philosopher could attain it thereafter.",incipit:{native:"Naturalis Philosophiae partem secretam, scientiam videlicet famosam, inclytam & gloriosam, insipientibus occultam, sed piis ac prudentibus manifestam scribimus, qua pauperibus & inopibus subveniri possit.",english:"We write of the secret part of natural philosophy — that famous, renowned and glorious science, hidden from the foolish but open to the pious and the prudent — by which the poor and the destitute may be relieved.",note:"The first sentence of Caput I; the promise that the art exists for the relief of the poor returns a page later as an argument for keeping it from kings and princes."},contents:null,contentsNote:"The treatise is divided into numbered capita — the 1602 printing runs at least as far as a fifty-fourth — but the numerals stand bare. Not one of the chapters carries a title or rubric of any kind, so that a table of contents would be nothing but a list of numbers, and none is offered here.",editions:[{label:"Theatrum Chemicum, vol. III (Oberursel: Cornelius Sutorius for Lazarus Zetzner, 1602)",url:"https://archive.org/details/BIUSante_pharma_res011287x03/page/n9/mode/1up",note:"The link opens at the treatise, which stands first in the volume, immediately after the elenchus of authors and tracts."},{label:"Theatrum Chemicum, vol. III (1602) — the whole volume",url:"https://archive.org/details/BIUSante_pharma_res011287x03",note:"The item page for the third volume entire, from which all four of the vol. III treatises listed here may be read online or downloaded."}]},{slug:"thesaurus-philosophiae",native:"Thesaurus philosophiae",translit:null,english:"The Treasury of Philosophy",author:"Efferarius Monachus (Efferarius the Monk), a medieval Latin alchemist known chiefly as the name attached to a small group of treatises, two of which stand together here.",date:"Medieval, date uncertain; printed in the Theatrum Chemicum, 1602",language:"Latin",volume:"Theatrum Chemicum, vol. III",description:"The second of the two pieces the Theatrum gives to Efferarius, printed directly beneath the Explicit of his De lapide philosophorum secundum verum modum eformando. It opens on the Creator who bound discordant things into concord, then turns to a confession of failure: the author has tried the work in blood, in hair and in eggs, been reduced to stupor, and given the mastery up — until Avicenna's question, 'If this thing is, how is it? and if it is not, how is it not?', sets him back on the road to mercury as the matter and sperm of all metals. Its later pages number the stages of the work as a life — the third is pregnancy, the fourth birth, the fifth nourishment — and quote the verses of Merculinus on the stone's many names: now silver, now gold, now element, now water, now wine, now blood.",incipit:{native:"Reserat nobis eius salutare, cuius sunt omnia, caelum, terra, mare, qui de nihilo cuncta creare potuit: & dissona consona ligare voluit: & aegra statuit medicinis curare ex sua bonitate.",english:"He to whom all things belong — heaven, earth and sea — unlocks his saving gift to us: he who was able to create all things out of nothing, who willed to bind discordant things into concord, and who of his own goodness ordained that sick things be healed by medicines.",note:"The treatise opens not with matter but with the Creator, and with the binding of discords — the figure under which the whole work will afterwards be described."},contents:null,contentsNote:"The Thesaurus runs continuously, without chapter divisions in the Theatrum Chemicum printing: no caput, section or numbered head occurs anywhere in it. The author does number the stages of the work within his running text, but the compositor set no headings for them, and the only breaks in the page are the short verse tags of Merculinus, set off in italic where they are quoted.",editions:[{label:"Theatrum Chemicum, vol. III (Oberursel: Cornelius Sutorius for Lazarus Zetzner, 1602)",url:"https://archive.org/details/BIUSante_pharma_res011287x03/page/n154/mode/1up",note:"The link opens at the page where the Thesaurus begins, in the lower half, directly under the Explicit of Efferarius's preceding treatise."}]},{slug:"practica-magistri-odomari",native:"Practica magistri Odomari ad discipulum",translit:null,english:"The Practice of Master Odomar, Addressed to His Disciple",author:"Odomar (Odomarus), of whom little is known beyond this piece. The volume styles him magister, and he addresses the work to a 'brother Ludovicus'.",date:"Medieval; printed in the Theatrum Chemicum, 1602",language:"Latin",volume:"Theatrum Chemicum, vol. III",description:"A short and wholly practical piece, in which a master breaks his oath of secrecy for love of a pupil and hands over, in an unbroken run of prose, a course of calcination, dissolution, distillation, sublimation and fixation for purified silver and sublimed mercury — repeated six times, then five — together with the projections it will bear, one part upon a hundred, then upon six thousand, then upon a thousand thousand, and at last upon numbers without limit. The white work is then turned red with rubified mercury and a sharp water of vitriol, saltpetre, cinnabar and Yemeni alum, after which the Theatrum prints under the same heading a handful of appended notes and recipes. That so slight a thing was thought worth reprinting says something about how the Theatrum was used: as a working shelf, and not only as a library.",incipit:{native:"Cogis me frater Ludovice contra omnium philosophorum praecepta militare: sed quia tui amore sanctissimo cor meum accenditur: ideo secretum quod hactenus pater filio negat, tibi carissimo fratri fideliter iungo.",english:"You compel me, brother Ludovicus, to campaign against the precepts of all the philosophers; but because my heart is kindled with a most holy love of you, I therefore faithfully make over to you, dearest brother, the secret that until now a father withholds from his son.",note:"The whole apology for breaking the oath of silence takes a single sentence; the recipe begins in the next words, 'Recipe Lunae purissimae…'."},contents:null,contentsNote:"The Practica proper carries no divisions: it runs as one continuous recipe from the address to Ludovicus to the final projection, without a break, a heading or a numbered paragraph. It is followed within the same item, and under the same running head, by several short appended pieces which do carry headings of their own — a note on guarding against poisonous alchemical fumes, an 'Arcanum Philosophorum' for making gold from Saturn, and a preparation of common salt for the philosophers' stone. No table of contents is offered, since these are appendages rather than divisions of the text.",editions:[{label:"Theatrum Chemicum, vol. III (Oberursel: Cornelius Sutorius for Lazarus Zetzner, 1602)",url:"https://archive.org/details/BIUSante_pharma_res011287x03/page/n172/mode/1up",note:"The link opens at the page where the Practica begins, about halfway down, immediately below the close of the Praxis universalis ascribed to Lull that precedes it."}]},{slug:"tractatus-de-marchasita",native:"Tractatus de marchasita",translit:null,english:"A Treatise on Marcasite",author:"Anonymous. The places the author names are mostly Italian, which suggests he wrote in or for Italy, though Bohemia and Bern are named among them.",date:"Date uncertain; printed in the Theatrum Chemicum, 1602",language:"Latin",volume:"Theatrum Chemicum, vol. III",description:"The full heading runs 'Tractatus de marchasita, ex qua tandem cum aliis dicendis fit elixir ad album' — a treatise on marcasite, from which at last, together with the other matters to be spoken of, the elixir to the white is made. Unusually among these tracts it opens as mineralogy rather than allegory: marcasite, which the Greeks call pyrites, is found in many kinds and many places, and the author lists them — above the city of Brindisi in Apulia near San Giovanni de Migento, in the Romagna at Britonorum, in Corsica near the castle called Canui at a place named Bagnaria, about the Baths of Porretta, out of Sardinia where true silver is dug at a place called Villa de Glexia, in Bohemia, near Bern where tin is mined, and in the district of Vicenza. Only then does he invoke the name of the most high Creator and our Lord Jesus Christ and begin to work. One detail may date the text more sharply than anything else in it: marcasite is explained as the stuff which, mixed with lead, makes the rods from which printers' types are cast — a comparison which, on the natural reading, cannot be older than the press, whatever the age of the rest of the tract.",incipit:{native:"Multorum generum & multis in locis reperitur Marchasita vulgo dicta, à Graecis autem Pyrites: est autem ex metalli genere, nam cum plumbo mista efficit notularum pro typis virgulas.",english:"What is commonly called marcasite — though the Greeks call it pyrites — is found in many kinds and in many places. It belongs to the class of metals, for mixed with lead it makes the rods from which the characters of printers' type are formed.",note:"The opening sentence defines its subject by trade rather than by symbol, and in doing so appears to betray a date: it explains marcasite by reference to the casting of type."},contents:null,contentsNote:"No divisions are printed. The treatise opens with its survey of the places where marcasite is found, then proceeds through its operations in unbroken paragraphs, marked only by the recipe formula 'R.' where a fresh preparation begins and by occasional inline transitions in the running text — 'Modus componendi medicinam talis est', 'Nota hunc alium modum' — which are sentences rather than headings.",editions:[{label:"Theatrum Chemicum, vol. III (Oberursel: Cornelius Sutorius for Lazarus Zetzner, 1602)",url:"https://archive.org/details/BIUSante_pharma_res011287x03/page/n180/mode/1up",note:"The link opens at the treatise's heading and the opening list of localities; it runs to scan image 183, where De arsenico follows."}]},{slug:"cheiragogia-heliana",native:"Cheiragogia Heliana de auro philosophico necdum cognito",translit:null,english:"The Helian Hand-Guide, concerning the Philosophical Gold Not Yet Known",author:"Nicolaus Niger Hapelius — a declared anagram, resolving to Raphael Eglinus Iconius",date:"Preface subscribed 4 April 1612; printed 1613",language:"Latin",volume:"Theatrum Chemicum, vol. IV (Strasbourg: Lazarus Zetzner, 1613), p. 299",description:"A 'cheiragogia' is a leading by the hand, and the author keeps his promise: he declares in the preface that this open treatise on the philosophical gold has been written 'not in the allegorical style of the chymists, as they are wont, but plainly, that the hitherto hidden Tincture of the Sun may be understood'. The treatise looks toward Elias the Artist — the preface hopes that things covered shall at last be made more open, 'Elias the Artist being at length brought into the light' — and it stands in the same volume beside the author's Aphorismi Basiliani sive Canones Hermetici, to which its closing Protestatio expressly defers, and beside the Nova disquisitio Heliae Artistae printed there at p. 241 under the kindred pseudonym Heliophilus a Percis. Written in the spring of 1612, it belongs to the years and to the expectation out of which the Rosicrucian manifestos emerged, though it makes no claim of fraternity.",incipit:{native:"[S]cribit Basilius Valentinus, ordinis Benedictini monachus, qui proavorum memoria, ex superiore Alsatia oriundus, philosophus in chymia fuit princeps, in Curru suo Triumphali Antimonii…",english:"Basil Valentine writes — a monk of the Benedictine order who, within our forefathers' memory, sprung from Upper Alsace, was a prince among philosophers in chymistry — in his Triumphal Chariot of Antimony…",note:"The opening of the treatise proper on p. 299 of the 1613 Theatrum Chemicum; the drop capital S is restored. The treatise begins by citing the Currus Triumphalis Antimonii on the stone of fire, which 'non tingit universaliter, sicut Lapis Philosophorum, qui ex Solis essentia paratur' — which does not tinge universally, as the Stone of the Philosophers does that is prepared from the essence of the Sun, but only particularly."},contents:null,contentsNote:"No table of contents is given because the work has none to give. In the Theatrum Chemicum printing the Cheiragogia runs continuously — an author's preface subscribed 'Perscripsi 4. April. Anno 1612', a short Protestatio, and then an unbroken discourse from p. 299 to its close, without chapter, section or aphorism headings. On the name: the Protestatio on p. 298 is subscribed with four initials, 'R. E. I. D.', and the letters of 'Nicolaus Niger Hapelius' — twenty-one of them — are exactly the letters of 'Raphael Eglinus Iconius', neither more nor fewer, as a letter count confirms. The identification is not therefore a guess but the book's own; the author declares the device himself, signing the preface to his Aphorismi Basiliani a few pages later (p. 326) as 'Nicolaus Niger Hapelius Anagrammatizomenos'.",editions:[{label:"Theatrum Chemicum, vol. IV (Strasbourg, 1613)",url:"https://archive.org/details/BIUSante_pharma_res011287x04",note:"The Zetzner quarto of 1613, digitised by the Bibliothèque interuniversitaire de santé, Paris. The Cheiragogia begins at p. 299; the volume's own elenchus lists it between Zanetinis's Conclusio (p. 277) and Lavinius's Tractatus de coelo terrestri. Full page images and searchable text."}]},{slug:"tractatus-de-coelo-terrestri",native:"Tractatus de coelo terrestri",translit:null,english:"Treatise on the Terrestrial Heaven",author:"Venceslaus Lavinius of Moravia (Václav Lavín z Ottenfeldu); the volume heads it simply 'Venceslai Lavinii Moravi'",date:"Printed 1613",language:"Latin",volume:"Theatrum Chemicum, vol. IV (Strasbourg: Lazarus Zetzner, 1613), pp. 324–325",description:"One of the shortest pieces in the volume, and one of the most quoted: two pages on a single corporeal spirit, first created and everywhere present, threefold in salt, sulphur and mercury, which preserves what is pure and good and destroys what is impure and evil. Its title is earned in a single sentence near the middle, where Lavinius explains that because this water is joined within the earth itself to heaven, 'hoc nomine pulchro illam voco coelum terrestre' — by this beautiful name I call it the terrestrial heaven. The doctrine that the sky is hidden in the ground, and may be drawn out of it by art imitating nature, passed from here into a great deal of later alchemical writing.",incipit:{native:"[V]nus est spiritus corporeus a Natura primo creatus, communis, occultus, vitae pretiosum: conservans pura et bona, impura et mala destruens.",english:"There is one corporeal spirit, first created by Nature, common to all, hidden, the precious thing of life: preserving what is pure and good, destroying what is impure and evil.",note:"The opening sentence as it stands on p. 324 of the 1613 quarto, with the ornamental drop capital V restored. The sheet is clean and the reading is not in doubt."},contents:null,contentsNote:"The treatise carries no internal divisions. It occupies two pages of the 1613 quarto, pp. 324–325, and runs as a single continuous discourse from its opening definition of the spirit to its close, without chapters, sections or numbered aphorisms.",editions:[{label:"Theatrum Chemicum, vol. IV (Strasbourg, 1613)",url:"https://archive.org/details/BIUSante_pharma_res011287x04",note:"The Zetzner quarto of 1613, digitised by the Bibliothèque interuniversitaire de santé, Paris. The treatise is headed 'Tractatus de coelo terrestri, Venceslai Lavinii Moravi' and falls between the Cheiragogia Heliana and the preface to Hapelius's Aphorismi Basiliani, which begins on p. 326."}]},{slug:"duodecim-tractatus-de-lapide-philosophorum",native:"Duodecim tractatus de lapide philosophorum",translit:null,english:"Twelve Treatises on the Philosophers' Stone",author:"'Divi Leschi Genus Amo' — an anagram of MICHAEL SENDIVOGIUS (Michał Sędziwój)",date:"1613 in the Theatrum Chemicum; also printed separately at Frankfurt, 1611",language:"Latin",volume:"Theatrum Chemicum, vol. IV (Strasbourg: Lazarus Zetzner, 1613), p. 478",description:"These twelve treatises are the Novum Lumen Chymicum itself — the book that carried Sendivogius's teaching across Europe, and which the separate Frankfurt title-page of 1611 describes as 'drawn from the fountain of nature and of manual experience'. Their argument moves in one unbroken line from what nature is, through the generation of metals and stones in the earth, to the practice by which art may imitate that generation; the twelfth closes on the stone and its virtue. That the series itself knew whose book this was is plain twice over: the Theatrum's sixth volume prints Orthelius's commentary 'on the Novum Lumen Chymicum of Michael Sendivogius the Pole', and in the fourth volume Hapelius, writing a hundred and eighty pages earlier, names the anagram and solves it outright.",incipit:{native:"[M]ulti sapientes doctissimique viri, ante complura secula, imo Hermete teste, ante diluvium, multa scripserunt de conficiendo lapide philosophorum, et tot nobis reliquerunt scripta…",english:"Many wise and most learned men, many ages ago — indeed, as Hermes bears witness, before the Flood — wrote much concerning the making of the philosophers' stone, and left us writings so many…",note:"The opening of Tractatus I, transcribed from the Frankfurt printing of 1611, whose sheets read more cleanly than the Theatrum's; the drop capital M is restored. The 1613 Theatrum text agrees word for word, though its impression of the second word is indistinct."},contents:[{heading:"Tractatus I. De natura, quidnam ea sit, et quales eius debeant esse scrutatores",english:"On Nature — what it is, and what manner of men its searchers ought to be"},{heading:"Tractatus II. De operatione naturae in nostro proposito et spermate",english:"On the working of Nature in our subject and in the sperm"},{heading:"Tractatus III. De vera prima metallorum materia",english:"On the true first matter of the metals"},{heading:"Tractatus IV. Quomodo metalla in terrae visceribus generantur",english:"How the metals are generated in the bowels of the earth"},{heading:"Tractatus V. De generatione lapidum omnis generis",english:"On the generation of stones of every kind"},{heading:"Tractatus VI. De secunda materia, et putrefactione rerum",english:"On the second matter, and on the putrefaction of things"},{heading:"Tractatus VII. De virtute secundae materiae",english:"On the virtue of the second matter"},{heading:"Tractatus VIII. De arte, et quomodo per artem operatur natura in semine",english:"On art, and how through art Nature works within the seed"},{heading:"Tractatus IX. De commixtione metallorum, vel de eliciendo semine metallico",english:"On the commixture of metals, or on drawing forth the metallic seed"},{heading:"Tractatus X. De generatione supra naturali filii Solis",english:"On the supernatural generation of the son of the Sun"},{heading:"Tractatus XI. De praxi, et confectione lapidis seu tincturae per artem",english:"On the practice, and the making of the stone or tincture by art"},{heading:"Tractatus XII. De lapide et eius virtute",english:"On the stone and its virtue"}],contentsNote:"The twelve headings above are transcribed from printed sheets, not reconstructed. All twelve stand clear in the separate Frankfurt octavo of 1611; eleven of them were then read again in the Theatrum Chemicum of 1613, the exception being the tenth, whose heading is not legible in that impression although its running head, 'De Generat. Supernaturali', is. Where the two witnesses differ the Theatrum's form is given here. On the anagram: 'Divi Leschi Genus Amo' and 'Michael Sendivogius' are each eighteen letters, and a letter-by-letter count returns exactly the same letters in the same numbers — A, C, D, E twice, G, H, I three times, L, M, N, O, S twice, U and V — with nothing left over on either side. The solution is also printed in the Theatrum itself: in the Cheiragogia Heliana, Hapelius turns to 'the most excellent author of the little book of the twelve Treatises of the Philosophers' Stone, whose anagram was QUI DIVI LESCHI GENUS AMO, that is, MICHAEL SENDIVOGIUS, that Pole'. Waite's English version of 1893 sets it out again, glossing the phrase 'I love the Divine Race of Leschi' and naming Sendivogius on the page.",editions:[{label:"Theatrum Chemicum, vol. IV (Strasbourg, 1613)",url:"https://archive.org/details/BIUSante_pharma_res011287x04",note:"The Zetzner quarto, digitised by the Bibliothèque interuniversitaire de santé, Paris. The twelve treatises begin at p. 478, headed 'Duodecim tractatus de lapide philosophorum. Authore me, qui Divi Leschi Genus Amo'; on p. 477 stands a commendatory epigram subscribed by Johann Heinrich Alsted at Frankfurt am Main, Easter 1611."},{label:"De lapide philosophorum tractatus duodecim (Frankfurt: Johann Bringer, 1611)",url:"https://archive.org/details/hin-wel-all-00003011-001",note:"A separate octavo printing, from the Wellcome collection, with the title-page attribution 'Author sum, qui Divi Leschi Genus Amo' and the descriptive phrase 'e naturae fonte et manuali experientia deprompti'. Its sheets are markedly cleaner than the Theatrum's, and it is the witness against which all twelve headings above were confirmed. The cataloguer likewise identifies the author as Sendivogius."},{label:"The New Chemical Light, in The Hermetic Museum, vol. II (London, 1893)",url:"https://archive.org/details/b24927363_0002",note:"A. E. Waite's English version, headed 'The New Chemical Light, drawn from the Fountain of Nature and of Manual Experience', to which is added a treatise concerning sulphur. It is here that the anagram is set out and solved in English."}]},{slug:"harmonia-seu-consensus-philosophorum-chemicorum",native:"Harmonia seu consensus philosophorum chemicorum",translit:null,english:"Harmony, or the Consensus of the Chemical Philosophers",author:"David Lagneus, physician (the volume styles him 'Davidis Lagnei D. Medici')",date:"1613",language:"Latin",volume:"Theatrum Chemicum, vol. IV (Strasbourg: Lazarus Zetzner, 1613), pp. 815–908",description:"Not a treatise but an instrument: a florilegium in which the sayings of the philosophers are cut loose from their books and set side by side under the heads of the work, so that their agreement may be seen rather than argued for. The head-title's claim is a proud one — arranged 'magno cum studio et labore in ordinem digestus, a nemine alio hac methodo distributus', with great study and labour, and by no one else distributed in this method — and the margins name the authorities as they speak: Hollandus, Grevelius, Alanus, Arnaldus, Rhasis, the Turba. It is the kind of book that made the Theatrum useful to working readers, since it does in ninety-odd pages what would otherwise take a shelf.",incipit:{native:"[S]apientes lapidi multa nomina indidere. Postquam materiam lapidis reclusissent ac spiritualem effecissent, dixere materiam esse rem vilem.",english:"The wise gave the stone many names. When they had unlocked the matter of the stone and made it spiritual, they said the matter was a vile thing.",note:"The opening of Cap. I, De mysterii physici nominibus, transcribed from the 1613 sheets with the drop capital S restored. The chapter proceeds through the roll of names — serpent, water, earth, virgin's milk, blood — each attached to the operation that earned it, the whole series credited in the margin to Johannes Isaacus Hollandus."},contents:null,contentsNote:"The Harmonia is genuinely divided into numbered chapters (capita), each gathering the philosophers' sayings on a single head of the work, and the Theatrum printing runs to at least Cap. XX. No list is given here because a complete and correct one cannot honestly be assembled: the 1613 sheets have taken enough damage that roughly half the chapter headings are no longer legible, and a sequence part-read and part-supplied would be worse than none. The following can be read with certainty and may be taken as a fair sample of the plan — Cap. I, De mysterii physici nominibus; Cap. II, An lapidis physici conficiendi plures sint viae; Cap. IV, Quid sumendum in lapide physico, qualiter; Cap. V, De remotione superflui in lapide physico; Cap. VI, De operationibus artis philosophorum; Cap. VII, De nutritione lapidis physici; Cap. VIII, De igne proprio lapidi physico; Cap. IX, De vase philosophico ad lapidem physicum conficiendum; Cap. XIV, Probatio operis physici perfecti; and Cap. XX, Haec ars divina quomodo acquiratur, et qualis debeat esse artifex.",editions:[{label:"Theatrum Chemicum, vol. IV (Strasbourg, 1613)",url:"https://archive.org/details/BIUSante_pharma_res011287x04",note:"The Zetzner quarto, digitised by the Bibliothèque interuniversitaire de santé, Paris. The Harmonia begins at p. 815 and closes at p. 908; a commendatory poem on the Harmonia by M. Quadratus, royal counsellor and physician, follows on pp. 909–910, and Albertus Magnus's De concordantia philosophorum in lapide begins at p. 911."}]},{slug:"elucidarius-artis-transmutatoriae",native:"Elucidarius artis transmutatoriae metallorum summa maior",translit:null,english:"The Elucidator: the Greater Summa of the Art of Transmuting Metals",author:"Christophorus Parisiensis (Christopher of Paris); the volume calls him 'philosophi vetustissimi Raimundi Lullii imitatoris'",date:"Printed 1661; the text itself is considerably older, its author described in the volume as a most ancient philosopher and an imitator of Ramon Llull",language:"Latin",volume:"Theatri chemici volumen sextum (Strasbourg: heirs of Eberhard Zetzner, 1661), p. 195",description:"The volume's own index calls this the 'summa maior' of the transmutatory art, and the description is just: it is a full course in three books, divided into theory and practice, from the twofold way of the stone through the chaos or first philosophical matter to the ordering of the medicines. Christopher writes in the Lullian manner and begins, as such books do, by complaining that his predecessors hid the art so enviously in obscure words that it lay nearly extinct for nine hundred years. The 1661 printing is enlarged with a double appendix, theoretical and practical, which the title-page declares had never before been seen; the practical one is headed as drawn from the author's French manuscripts.",incipit:{native:"[I]n nomine Domini nostri Iesu Christi filii Dei vivi Salvatoris nostri incipio scribere Elucidarium nobilissimae artis, qua metalla imperfecta ex substantia et forma sua in meliorem transmutantur.",english:"In the name of our Lord Jesus Christ, Son of the living God, our Saviour, I begin to write the Elucidation of that most noble art by which imperfect metals are transmuted, out of their own substance and form, into a better.",note:"The opening of the author's preface, transcribed from the 1661 sheets with the drop capital I restored."},contents:[{heading:"Praefatio authoris",english:"The author's preface, on the envious obscurity of the older writers and the near extinction of the art for nine hundred years"},{heading:"Elenchus rerum quae in hoc opusculo continentur",english:"The printed table of what the little work contains, setting out its division into three books — the first teaching the theory, the other two the practice"},{heading:"Elucidarii liber primus, in VII capita divisus — Cap. I, De lapidis philosophorum seu tincturae duplici via",english:"Book One, divided into seven chapters: the theory, opening on the twofold way of the stone or tincture"},{heading:"Appendix theorica, pro meliore praecedentium declaratione",english:"A theoretical appendix, for the better declaring of what has gone before; it begins with a definition of alchemy as the art that administers, in this excellent magistery, the necessary means — 'fornaces, vasa, mensurae et ignes ordinati', furnaces, vessels, measures and ordered fires"},{heading:"Elucidarii liber secundus, seu Practica scientiae arboris philosophalis — Cap. I, De chao seu materia prima philosophica, et de loco ubi Mercurius philosophorum inveniatur, eiusque diversitate",english:"Book Two, or the Practice of the Science of the Philosophical Tree: beginning with the chaos or first philosophical matter, and where the Mercury of the philosophers is to be found, and its diversity"},{heading:"Elucidarii liber III, seu tertia pars Elucidarii — De ordine medicinarum, cum reprobatione sophisticationum Geberi",english:"Book Three, or the third part: on the order of the medicines, with a refutation of Geber's sophistications"},{heading:"Appendix practica, antehac nunquam visa, Christophori Parisiensis manuscriptis Gallicis desumpta",english:"A practical appendix, never before seen, drawn from Christopher of Paris's French manuscripts, and closing with the medical elixir and a description of potable gold"}],contentsNote:"These divisions are transcribed from the headings as they stand in the 1661 printing, and the work is unusual among Theatrum pieces in carrying its own printed table, an Elenchus rerum, against which they can be checked. That table states the plan exactly: the whole consists of three books, the first teaching the Theorica in seven chapters, the second and third the Practica; the second book, called the Arbor Sapientum or Arbor scientiae philosophalis, is divided into fourteen chapters; the third contains three ordines, of four, five and six members, the last being the Universale, 'quod est cor Philosophorum'; and the practical appendix holds seven numbered items, ending with a tract of the philosopher De la Brosse dated 1545. Only the first chapter of Books One and Two is named above, since the chapter headings inside the three books are numerous and many are worn in the scan. The 'double appendix' advertised on the title-page is the theoretical and the practical appendix together, and the book says so at their close: 'Sic igitur utraque huius Elucidarii appendix'.",editions:[{label:"Theatri chemici volumen sextum (Strasbourg, 1661)",url:"https://archive.org/details/BIUSante_pharma_res011287x06",note:"The sixth volume of the Theatrum, translated out of German and French into Latin by Johann Jacob Heilmann of Zweibrücken and digitised by the Bibliothèque interuniversitaire de santé, Paris. The Elucidarius is the fourth of the volume's twelve treatises, entered in the elenchus as 'Christophori Parisiensis Elucidarius seu artis transmutatoriae summa maior cum appendice', and runs from p. 195 to p. 293."}]},{slug:"arca-arcani-artificiosissimi",native:"Arca arcani artificiosissimi de summis naturae mysteriis",translit:null,english:"The Ark of the Most Artful Secret, concerning the Highest Mysteries of Nature",author:"Johannes Grasseus, called Chortalasseus (Johann Grasshoff, d. 1623), doctor of both laws and syndic of the city of Stralsund",date:"Printed 1661; the second part is subscribed as completed in 1598, old style",language:"Latin",volume:"Theatri chemici volumen sextum (Strasbourg: heirs of Eberhard Zetzner, 1661), pp. 294–396",description:"The Ark is built, as its title-page says, out of Grasseus's own Greater and Lesser Rusticus — the two 'Bauer' tracts he had put out in German — together with a Round Natural Philosophy set down 'per visionem Cabalisticam chemicam', through a chemical cabalistic vision, which opens with the Sun and Moon appearing with the whole firmament, halting in their course, coloured but giving no light. It is a book that keeps insisting the art is a gift rather than a conquest: before a word of practice it assembles testimonies from James, Paul, Alanus, Alphidius, Dionysius Zacharias and Hermes, and then says why — 'I have inserted these testimonies here to this end, that no one should presume to accomplish this work by his own effort alone.' Two letters of the Fraternity R. C. are printed among the closing pieces.",incipit:{native:"Benedictio est ab eo qui est principium et finis.",english:"The blessing is from him who is the beginning and the end.",note:"The motto standing at the head of the treatise in the 1661 printing, immediately beneath the title and before the series of scriptural and philosophical testimonies with which the book opens. The author's own discourse begins only after them, with 'Haec testimonia hunc in finem hic inserui'."},contents:[{heading:"De arcano artificiosissimo in genere",english:"On the most artful secret, treated generally — the author sets out the whole series before descending to particulars, since from generals the special things are more easily known"},{heading:"Pars secunda: Lilium inter spinas",english:"Part Two: The Lily among Thorns, opening on Solomon's prayer for wisdom in Wisdom 7, and subscribed as happily finished in the citadel of the Sun in the year of salvation 1598, old style"},{heading:"Praxis authoris Johannis Grassei J.U.D., Stralsundensium syndici, qui se Chortalasseum vocavit",english:"The practice of the author, Johann Grasseus, doctor of both laws and syndic of Stralsund, who called himself Chortalasseus; it breaks off with the note 'Reliqua desunt', the rest is wanting"},{heading:"Figura cabalistica; Physica naturalis rotunda visionis chemicae cabalisticae",english:"The cabalistic figure, and the Round Natural Philosophy of the chemical cabalistic vision, which begins with the Sun and Moon appearing with the whole firmament"},{heading:"De generatione hominis et animalium",english:"On the generation of man and of animals — a chapter within the Round Natural Philosophy, introduced with the promise that we shall now perceive how man is governed and born according to the stars"},{heading:"Appendix de via ad aurum potabile perveniendi",english:"Appendix, by an anonymous philosopher, on the way to attain potable gold"},{heading:"Epistola F.R.C.; altera responsio",english:"Letters of the Fraternity R.C., the second beginning with the writer entering the seven circles of heaven and touching the highest with his thoughts while standing with his feet on the lowest"}],contentsNote:"These are the major divisions as they are printed in the sixth volume of the Theatrum, each transcribed from its heading in the sheets. Four cautions belong with the list. The final appendix on potable gold is by an anonymous hand and not by Grasseus — the title-page says so explicitly, 'quibus accessit appendix anonymi cuiusdam philosophi'. Only the second part carries a numbered part-heading, the first being implied rather than labelled. 'Cabala chemica' is not a division of the book but the running head that stands over the whole exposition of the vision, from the Physica naturalis rotunda onward, and it is therefore not listed as a section here. And no heading 'De consensu philosophorum' occurs in the treatise, though the consensus of the philosophers is invoked in its prose. On the name: the printing settles it. The volume's elenchus enters the work under 'Johannis Grassei Chortalassei dicti', the heading of the Praxis says outright that this is the man 'qui se Chortalasseum vocavit', and the title-page gives the variant 'alias Cortalassei'. It is thus a Graecised form of Grasshoff chosen by the author himself; its first element is evidently the Greek χόρτος, which carries both senses the German surname needs, 'grass, fodder' and 'enclosed yard, farmyard'.",editions:[{label:"Theatri chemici volumen sextum (Strasbourg, 1661)",url:"https://archive.org/details/BIUSante_pharma_res011287x06",note:"The sixth volume of the Theatrum, digitised by the Bibliothèque interuniversitaire de santé, Paris. The Arca is the fifth of the volume's twelve treatises, beginning at p. 294 and running to p. 396, where Orthelius's commentary on Sendivogius takes over at p. 397."},{label:"Aperta arca arcani artificiosissimi (in German, Leipzig, 1658)",url:"https://archive.org/details/b32991332_0002",note:"The German form of the same work — 'the opened and open-standing chest of the greatest and most artful secrets of nature, of the greater and lesser Bauer' — catalogued under Johann Grasshoff, d. 1623, and printed at Leipzig by C. Müller. Its subtitle names in German the two Rusticus tracts out of which the Latin Arca was constructed."}]}]};export{e as default};
